ing is that i  always follow my interests with books  and i very often  go down rabbit holes if i find something  interesting i will very guiltlessly read  it i've  read completely trashy books i've read  classics i've read  books about deriving physical formulas  so literally anything that i really care  about i go where my interests  take me when i was younger a huge amount  of what i read would just be  encyclopedias and i would go from one  encyclopedia to the next to the next to  the next and just  read a whole book about mummification in  ancient egypt and then go down another  rabbit hole and read about the process  of mummification on the body and then go  down another rabbit hole and read about  human biology and then go down another  rabbit hole and read about the specific  organ  so i think just following what interests  you and going down that rabbit hole  is such an easy way to read more and  find reading fun and my third point it  kind of feeds on that and that is always  going back to first principles  if i read one book it may be one chapter  or even one sentence in a book that  really really resonates with me a great  example of this are stephen hawking's  books on the universe  so i will read something about black  holes for example and it will have a  formula which really resonates with me  and i think oh this is really cool what  is the physics behind this so then i'll  go to a physics textbook and read about  that  and then i'll go into a deeper physics  textbook like a university book  that talks about that specific maths i  know this is super nerdy and not what  i'm recommending for  anything this is just because i'm really  into physics but let's say you are into  literature  you can go down the same rabbit holes  with different authors you can go down  these rabbit holes with history  you can easily go down these rabbit  holes with archaeology literally  anything you are interested in and it  only takes one thing that can prompt you  for what book to go to next and you can  easily  form a reading list for yourself super  super simply the first point is that i  don't really care  about what is hot at the minute or what  is cool for example i never read 50  shades of grey or twilight or the hunger  games  if a book doesn't interest me it just  doesn't and because it's mainstream may  mean that it's more accessible but if it  doesn't resonate with you  i think that it can add to you not  liking books if you're trying to read  what all your friends are reading and  saying is great  and is not right you might feel that oh  i guess i'm just not a reader then but  that's not necessarily the case so i  sometimes think that avoiding mainstream  books might actually be a good idea  and finding your niche or finding people  with whom you resonate with especially  online that's super easy to do now and  see what their favorite books are you're  much more likely to  find books that are more related to you  for example i've read time enough for  love it's a very niche book which is now  one of my favorite books and i heard it  on a podcast  of someone on tim ferriss so it was just  this one scientist on tim ferriss with  whom i thought oh  i really love this and um yeah that's  how i get my book recommendations  rather than going to like the top  bestsellers on kindle for example  which i might actually not find that  cool my fifth point is that i  reread books a lot i don't really care  about how many books i read as a number  and my goal isn't to kind of just read  as much as possible  my goal is to have as much fun as  possible and sometimes a book is so good  that i will want to read it like a  hundred times i remember when i was  younger i  definitely did this with harry potter so  i read each of the harry potter books  seven times each and i would just go  through them again  and again and again especially when i  was waiting for the new books to come  out  shout out to anyone who is from that  generation but i think this is a huge  recommendation of mine if you're just  not finding new books that you like  or if you haven't read in a long time  what is a book from your childhood that  you really enjoyed a book that you read  a long time ago that you really enjoyed  just go back to it because it doesn't  just have to be new books  although i do get fomo and i wish i  could live forever so that i could read  every book in the world it doesn't  really matter if i like something i will  reread it i will enjoy it again and  again  i felt so reassured when i heard nabal  which is one of the people that i  respect the most in my life um saying  that he sometimes goes halfway through  the book before he realizes that he's  read it before  and i honestly felt the same but i felt  so stupid for saying it out loud  as though people might judge me that  well you clearly haven't read it well  enough the first time  but genuinely what he said is that when  you reread a book  and um then only later you realize that  you've read it before it's because it's  the right time for you to reapproach  this  and for the book to come into your life  and that's kind of the way that i see it  now  so both unintentionally and  intentionally i will read books again  and again  and re-enjoy them especially when i know  that it's a guaranteed pleasure for me  to read  just go back to it this can be novels  educational books anything really my  sixth point is that i take a lot of  notes  although i kind of recommend this to  everyone i know that it's also something  advanced and i'm  massively over the top with this in that  i literally have a second brain  and i um take notes of everything that i  read  to a huge extent i just find this a lot  of fun because it means that i can  reapply a lot of what i learn it's much  less likely for me to forget that i've  read that book  and also i can take the applications and  the notes and use them in other things  such as this video i'll show something  from a book later in this video  and also other things i produce i will  often pull information from those books  and connect them with each other  so note taking kind of means that you're  more active in engaging with the book  you're more active in general than just  possibly getting information  so definitely something that i would  recommend and the seventh point is that  i don't feel guilty about my approach at  all  sometimes i usually have three four five  maybe even ten books that i'm reading  at once in a way sometimes it takes me  one afternoon to read a book  sometimes i read two three different  books at different points in the day  sometimes i will sit down and binge read  a book very very slowly for  a week and read nothing else in that  time i just read  the way that i feel and kind of it's i  feel that it is my space to be  free and creative and whatever i feel  like doing in the moment is what i will  do  with as little guilt as possible so i  think in general it's just ties to  everything that i said  making it pleasurable will make it more  likely that you enjoy it  and one way to make it pleasurable is to  remove these obligations  you don't have to read shakespeare if it  genuinely doesn't resonate with you  it might not be right for you right now  and you don't need to read mainstream  books  and you don't need to finish every book  that you read if you want to make it  more fun for yourself  just doing what you feel like in the  moment when you feel like it will be the  best and i just wanted to  um add one one quote from allah in his  book on how perhaps can change your life  which kind of says something along the  lines that the reason that we might feel  lonely  is because our experiences are so  scattered  among the world there's actually a lot  of people who might be feeling very  similarly to how you feel  but not all of them are alive right now  not all of them  are in your vicinity and not all of them  are even expressing these things to you  but reading books can unlock so many  people who are going through the same  thing  that it just kind of makes you not feel  alone and  also gives you so much pleasure in  connecting with the universe and with  life  in a way that is so private but also  so communal that it's absolutely  ridiculous so  yeah on that note those were my top  recommendations  on how to make yourself fall in love  with books i honestly hope that this was  helpful in any shape or form  i would love i would genuinely  absolutely love  any sort of book recommendations that  you think might be a fit for me  or that you particularly enjoy a lot but  yeah thank you so much for spending this  time with me i hope you have a wonderful  rest of your day  behind yourself and others and don't  believe everything you think bye  you

